impact:
`package.json` declares `"prepack": "npm run build"` and `"prepare": "npm run build"`, which contradicts the standing hard constraint that this project ships no lifecycle scripts. The launcher design in T-011 exists *because* of that constraint (npm 12 / pnpm 10 block dependency scripts, so nothing can be set up at install time).

## CORRECTION 2026-08-04 — this is NOT the cheap, independent fix it was recorded as

An earlier handover listed this as "cheap and independent — `prepack`/`prepare` can be removed without touching any of the above". That is wrong, and the correction matters because the cost estimate is what decided its sequencing.

**The shipped packaging contract ASSERTS THE SCRIPTS EXIST.** `tests-ts/contract/packaging.contract.mjs:349-353`:

```
test("declares prepare as well as prepack", () => {
  // prepack does NOT run for `npm install <git-url>`, so prepack alone would leave a
  // ...
  assert.equal(MANIFEST.scripts.prepack, "npm run build");
  assert.equal(MANIFEST.scripts.prepare, "npm run build");
});
```

So deleting the two scripts turns a green suite red, and the test is not incidental — it was written deliberately, with a recorded reason: `prepack` alone does not cover `npm install <git-url>`, so `prepare` was added to close that path. The contract also relies on `npm pack` running `prepack` to prove the tarball cannot be cut from a stale `dist` (line 134).

Removing the scripts therefore requires all of: delete both scripts, replace the build guarantee they provided (an explicit release/CI build step plus whatever proves `dist` is not stale at pack time), amend or replace `packaging.contract.mjs:349-353` and the staleness argument at line 134, and keep the git-URL install path honest or declare it unsupported.

## Second gap, same constraint: nothing shipping enforces it

The constraint is a property of the whole installed tree, not just our manifest, and **no shipped check enforces it.** `packaging.contract.mjs` has no lifecycle-script scan of the dependency closure at all — it asserts `--ignore-scripts` was passed to the consumer install (lines 363-408), which proves the install did not RUN scripts, not that no package DECLARES them. The real lifecycle/native-closure inspector exists only under `spikes/mcp/` (`supply-chain.mjs`), and `spikes/` is classified by this repo as evidence, not shipping infrastructure.

This blocks T-013's dependency step: adding `@modelcontextprotocol/server@2.0.0` and `zod@4.4.3` to the root manifest under a no-lifecycle-scripts constraint that nothing shipping can verify would be an unchecked claim. The T-009 inspector needs promoting to a maintained root contract covering root scripts, every transitive package manifest, `gypfile` and `binding.gyp`, with install scripts disabled during inspection.

## Consequence for sequencing

T-013 cannot merge in a state that violates the constraint, so this is T-013's prerequisite as much as T-011's — whichever lands first carries it. It is no longer a loose end that can be picked up between tickets.

